A||||15||lennyb4||4.3 liter V-6||||5||
Z||000000||lennyb4||05-22-2000||01:14 PM||||I hear alot of people doing the 3.1 or the 3800 conversions. But has anyone ever or does anyone know if a 4.3 V-6 would be a good swap? Im going to look at a 1992 (i think) chevy van (needs front end work) as a run around vechicle for my wife. It has a 4.3 in it (65,000 miles) and if its a good transplant subject then im gonna swipe it and put a 305 (or whatever) in the van... TIA||||reg||5
Z||000001||deceler8||05-22-2000||01:17 PM||||I understand there's as much work involved as putting in a V8...but at least it all fits in the engine compartment.||||reg||1
Z||000002||Raydar||05-22-2000||05:04 PM||||Lenny,<BR>The 4.3 has the same tranny bolt pattern as the V-8, but the balancing is all different. You'll have to get a flywheel custom made. It also vibrates like hell. I've got a 4.3 in my Sonoma and at certain revs, it resonates. You can really feel it in the shifter. Using the kind of mounts that will probably be required by a swap (solid or urethane), you're probably gonna feel it all through the car. The 93 (I think) and later TBI 4.3, and the 94 (I think) and later CPI 4.3 have balance shafts. I don't know how much difference it makes. <BR>They *do* have a bunch of torque, though.<BR>Several folks (including me) have talked about this swap, but I've never heard of one being done.<BR>Good luck if you decide to try it. Please keep us posted.<P>Raydar||||reg||1
Z||000003||Monkeyman||05-22-2000||05:09 PM||||Might be a pretty tight fit, too.  The 4.3 is a 90* motor compared to the 2.8s 60*.  Don't know if that would make a difference or not.||||reg||1
Z||000004||terryk||05-22-2000||06:34 PM||||The Buick 3800 is a 90 degree. No reason the 4.3L wouldn't fit. The only car 4.3L was the Caprice TBI. The only nice 4.3L are the truck engines. Syclone would be nice...||||reg||1
Z||000005||2birds||05-22-2000||11:55 PM||||I had a 4.3 with throttle body injection in a 85 Monte Carlo.  Good engine, simple emissions control system (similar to 2.8), but Raydar is right about the vibration.  Mine had the lockup metric 3-speed automatic, no overdrive, and 70 mph was teeth rattling.  Since the Buick 3.8 is almost a bolt-in, for all the work involved in adapting it, I think the V-8 would be a better choice, since the 4.3 is basically 3/4 of a 350 chevy, and you would need a V-8 chevy adpter kit for the 4.3<BR>My humble opinion...||||reg||1
Z||000006||Jay||05-23-2000||12:45 AM||||There is a guy here in Toronto that has done the 4.3 install and let me tell you it goes faster than snot.  I'll try to get his e-mail address or get some photos for ya||||reg||1
Z||000007||HYPTNOTiSE||05-23-2000||08:40 AM||||I could see doing a 4.3 for only one reason really. The keep the car a v-6. To do this swap it involves practically everything that a v-8 swap does. Why not do a v-8? Well for this main reason.  At the drag strip you could run in the naturally aspirated v-6 class, have a huge avaliblity for after market speed parts.(4.3 is a sbc with two cylinders cut off)With an engine that could produce 250-300hp, and gobbs of torque, i dont think you'd have any problem beating just about any other NA v-6 compact car. ||||reg||1
Z||000008||lennyb4||05-23-2000||09:02 AM||||Well like i stated earlier the only reason i was thinking about the 4.3 is because i just bought (last night) a chevy conversion van with it in it. Was tempted to swap it into my fiero. Does anyone know what kind of ponnies and torque this thing produces? Even in a van its got some cahonna's.||||reg||1
Z||000009||Oreif||05-23-2000||09:45 AM||||I'm not sure what HP your engine has but I do know the 1992 TB injected ones were 180hp and the 1994 Jimmy 4X4 we use to own had regular fuel injection (called a "Vortec") it was suppose to be at 200hp. The reason it feels like a fast engine is the 4.3's have mucho torque.||||reg||1
Z||000010||Raydar||05-23-2000||11:04 AM||||Lenny,<BR>The TBI 4.3 in my Sonoma is rated at 160-170 HP, if I remember correctly. Don't know about torque rating, but the a$$-o-meter says that it is substantial. At expressway speeds, I can accelerate to pass in 5th gear. Don't need to downshift. (Also has a *lovely* rumble through a dual-out Flowmaster.)   <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/smile.gif"><p>[This message has been edited by Raydar (edited 05-23-2000).]||||reg||1
Z||000011||mshill||05-23-2000||01:04 PM||||I have a 4.3L HO in my GMC Safari van and a 4.3L (Holley TBI, Edelbrock intake, 1.6:1 roller rockers) in my boat.  Both these engines put out some mind numbing torque.<P>The boat motor was originally rated at 175HP with a weak 2bbl carb.  The 4bbl carbuerated engines are spec'ed at 225HP.  With the mods <BR>mentioned above I had to go up 3 inches in pitch on the prop and gained 5 mph on the top end which corresponds to a 40-50 HP gain.<P>If I was goint to go with one of these I would get something like a 94 or later.<BR>||||reg||1
Z||000012||Carrolles||05-23-2000||01:05 PM||||I've got the 4.3 Vortec in my 94 Safari van and it is very nice.  It is a much nicer combination then the stock 4.3 I had in an 85 Safari van.<P>One reason to opt for the 4.3 over the v-8 is not having to put the goofy looking water pump pulley through the fender well.  Although I don't think there are nearly as many go-fast parts for the 4.3.||||reg||1
Z||000013||Carrolles||05-23-2000||01:09 PM||||I know I'll probably get royally flamed and kicked off the forum for what I'm about to say but I'd sure like to transplant the 3.0 liter Duratec 24v OHC v-6 from my 97 Sable into my Fiero.  It pulls much harder than my newly re-built 2.8.<P>Now you can through me into the fire with all the "ricers" if you want.||||reg||1
Z||000014||rogergarrison||05-23-2000||02:40 PM||||you can get anything in there you want if you have the desire. A friend has a Lambo kitcar on a 87 Fiero chassis. it has a dual 4 bbl 454 chevy and runs like a bat outta u know where, just handles like a dump truck. info only||||reg||1
Z||000015||LarryB||05-23-2000||04:40 PM||||4.3L in the '95 Blazer was rated at 195HP and 260 ft-lbs of torque, I use mine to tow a 3000 lb boat, way more grunt than my '87 w/2.8L and 4.10 rear end and I get better gas milage to boot. <P>As a comparison the 305 in the C/K pickup those years w/o Vortec heads was rated at 175HP and IIRC 285 ft-lbs. ||||reg||1
